%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_25410EB4] ## ![](text:image:3bd7)
%[text]{"align":"center"} An Adelie penguin caring for her recently hatched [chick](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manchots_Ad%C3%A9lie_couvant.jpg).
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_04BAFB5C] ## Load and set up necessary data:
filename = "penguins.csv"; %[control:filebrowser:90e9]{"position":[12,26]}
penguins = readtable(filename);
penguins.species = categorical(penguins.species);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_829F593F] ## Problem 1:
%[text] Use the `pie()` function to make a chart that shows the percentage of males and females overall in this dataset. You will first need to use `categorical()` to make a categorical array of `penguins.sex`.




%[text] ![](text:image:1c1d) Reflect on what the distribution of males and females in the population might tell you about this dataset.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8BA1ED8E] ## Problem 2:
%[text] Create a scatter plot of bill depth versus body mass for all penguins. Include an x-label, y-label, and title:





%[text] ![](text:image:2512) How many distinct groups do you see in your plot?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_76814424] ## Problem 3:
%[text] Investigate what the cause of the distinct groups from Problem 2 might be.
%[text] Use `gscatter()` to plot bill depth versus body mass grouped by sex. Include an x-label, y-label, and title:





%[text] Use `gscatter()` again to plot bill depth versus body mass grouped by species:





%[text] ![](text:image:2ca8) Write a sentence or two about what this tells you about the relationship between bill depth and body mass for these penguins.
%[text] ***Bonus:*** Go back to your species graph and plot the species with different symbols.
%%
